From: DIR-842 R1 port
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: rtl8365mb: let the chip settle after a reset

rtl8365mb_reset_chip() waits only for the hardware reset bit to clear and then
returns straight into rtl8365mb_switch_init(), which blasts the vendor jam
table at the chip over the SMI bus -- bit-banged over two GPIOs on this board.
Realtek's own documentation says the chip needs a full second to reset, and the
reset bit clears well before that.

Writes issued into that window are dropped silently: no NAK, no error return.
Setup reports success and the switch is left partly configured, with whichever
registers happened to land. On the DIR-842 R1 that shows up as a box which
receives perfectly and forwards nothing out of any jack on a large fraction of
cold boots -- the switch's CPU port counts the bytes arriving from the SoC, no
egress counter moves anywhere, no discard counter moves either, and every
register sampled afterwards reads exactly what a working boot reads. Which
writes are lost varies from boot to boot, which is why the failure looks
intermittent and why no runtime knob recovers it.

Sleep out the documented settle before returning.

--- a/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/dsa/realtek/rtl8365mb_main.c
@@ -2991,6 +2991,7 @@
 static int rtl8365mb_reset_chip(struct realtek_priv *priv)
 {
 	u32 val;
+	int ret;
 
 	priv->write_reg_noack(priv, RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_REG,
 			      FIELD_PREP(RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_HW_MASK, 1));
@@ -2999,9 +3000,22 @@
 	 * for 100 ms before accessing any registers to prevent ACK timeouts.
 	 */
 	msleep(100);
-	return regmap_read_poll_timeout(priv->map, RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_REG, val,
-					!(val & RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_HW_MASK),
-					20000, 1e6);
+	ret = regmap_read_poll_timeout(priv->map, RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_REG, val,
+				       !(val & RTL8365MB_CHIP_RESET_HW_MASK),
+				       20000, 1e6);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* The reset bit clears long before the chip is ready to be programmed.
+	 * Realtek documents a full second for the reset, so wait out the rest
+	 * of it: register writes issued into this window are dropped silently,
+	 * with no NAK and no error return, and a switch that came up with part
+	 * of its init jam table missing gives no indication of it beyond not
+	 * forwarding.
+	 */
+	msleep(1000);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int rtl8365mb_setup(struct dsa_switch *ds)
